  "writeup_markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Tamil\"\nsubtitle: \"India\"\nregion: \"South Asia\"\ntags: [ethnography, south-asia]\n---\n\n## At a glance\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Who | The Tamils, also known as Tamilar, are a Dravidian ethnic group. |\n| Where | Mainly Tamil Nadu and Puducherry in southern India, with large populations in Sri Lanka, Malaysia and Singapore. |\n| How many | About 5.7% of India's population; 15.3% of Sri Lanka, 7% of Malaysia and 5% of Singapore. |\n| Language | Tamil. |\n| Religion | Mainly Hinduism, alongside Christianity, Islam, Jainism and Buddhism. |\n| Known for | Tamil literature · Temple architecture and sculpture · Music and dance · Rice-based cuisine · Religious festivals |\n\n## Overview\n\nThe Tamils are a Dravidian people with more than 3,800 years of continuous human occupation recorded in Tamil Nadu. They speak Tamil, one of the world's longest-surviving classical languages. Tamil culture is known for literature, temples, sculpture, music, dance, food and religious practice. Tamil-speaking communities also live across South Asia, Southeast Asia, the Middle East, Africa, the Caribbean and the Western World.\n\n## Material culture\n\nTamil material culture includes silk and cotton clothing, painted textiles, grand temples, bronze images, practical wooden and metal tools, and gold jewelry.\n\n### Textile & pattern traditions\n\nTamil textile traditions include decorated paintings, silk saris and regional cotton saris.\n\n- **Thanjavur painting** — This 16th-century art uses a cloth base, dyes, semi-precious stones and silver or gold threads.\n- **Kanchipuram silk sari** — This silk sari from Kanchipuram is worn by many South Indian women for weddings and other special occasions.\n- **Kovai Cora cotton** — This is a cotton sari made in the Coimbatore region.\n- **Flower hair pattern** (*veni*) — Veni is a pattern of flowers placed in the hair.\n\n### Clothing & dress\n\nTamil dress includes draped saris, wrapped men's garments and colorful rural lungis.\n\n- **Sari** — Women wrap this long garment around the waist and over one shoulder, usually leaving the midriff uncovered.\n- **Long skirt** (*pavaadai*) — Young girls wear this long skirt with a shorter sari.\n- **Short sari** (*dhavani*) — Young girls wear this shorter sari with a pavaadai.\n- **Men's wrap garment** (*veshti*) — Men wrap this rectangular, often white cloth around the legs and knot it at the waist.\n- **Rural wrap garment** (*lungi*) — This tubular garment has stitched ends and colorful batik patterns and is common in the countryside.\n\n### Architecture\n\nDravidian temples are large complexes built around a sanctum, towers, halls and water features.\n\n- **Sanctum** (*garbhagriha*) — The central shrine room is topped by a pyramidal tower.\n- **Pyramidal tower** (*vimana*) — This tower rises above the temple sanctum.\n- **Porch** (*mantapa*) — These porches lead toward the temple sanctum.\n- **Gate-pyramid** (*gopuram*) — These ornate entrance towers stand around temple enclosures and are topped by finials.\n- **Mahabalipuram monuments** — This Pallava complex from the 7th and 8th centuries contains more than forty rock-cut temples, monoliths and rock reliefs.\n\n### Ceramics, metalwork & everyday objects\n\nTamil objects include temple sculpture, bronze images, ritual vessels and practical tools.\n\n- **Chola bronze statue** — These statues were made by the lost-wax process using beeswax, sal tree resin, clay and molten metal.\n- **Throwing blade** (*valari*) — This wooden object has a flattened round handle and a curved blade widening toward its flat tip.\n- **Water vessel** — A brass vessel was used to carry water.\n- **Betel box** — A wooden box was used for betel.\n- **Trident** (*triśūla*) — This metal trident is a recorded Tamil object.\n\n### Jewelry & body adornment\n\nTamil adornment includes gold jewelry, bell anklets and flowers woven into the hair.\n\n- **Jasmine bud necklace** (*Malligai Arumbu Malai*) — This 19th-century necklace is made of gold and rubies strung on black thread.\n- **Gold ear pendant** (*Kathija*) — Kathija is a gold ear pendant.\n- **Bell anklets** (*salangai*) — These special anklets contain small bells and are worn with ceremonial dress.\n- **Flower hair arrangement** (*veni*) — Women plait their hair in a particular way and decorate it with flowers in a veni pattern.\n\n## Music & performance\n\nTamil music includes ancient systems, temple hymns, folk storytelling, percussion groups and Carnatic concerts.\n\n- **Tamil musical system** (*Tamil Pannisai*) — This was the musical system of ancient Tamil country.\n- **Temple reed instrument** (*nadaswaram*) — This reed instrument is often played with the thavil in temples and weddings.\n- **Temple drum** (*thavil*) — This drum commonly accompanies the nadaswaram.\n- **Percussion ensemble** (*melam*) — Melam is a group of percussion instruments played at events and functions.\n- **Bow-song storytelling** (*Villu Paatu*) — This form mixes spoken storytelling with music from a string bow and other instruments.\n\n## Dance & theatre\n\nTamil performance includes classical dance, folk dances and open-air theatre and puppetry.\n\n- **Classical dance** (*Bharatanatyam*) — This dance from Tamil Nadu uses bent legs, footwork, hand gestures, mudras and expressive eyes and faces.\n- **Pot-balancing dance** (*Karakattam*) — Dancers balance decorated pots on their heads.\n- **Stick dance** (*Kolattam*) — Dancers perform this dance with crossed sticks.\n- **Peacock dance** (*Mayilattam*) — This dance imitates a peacock.\n- **Street theatre** (*Koothu*) — Koothu combines dance, music, narration and singing in open public places during festivals.\n\n## Festivals & rituals\n\nTamil festivals honor harvests, water, gods, cattle and the Tamil calendar, while temples hold village-deity rituals.\n\n- **Harvest festival** (*Pongal*) — This multi-day festival in Thai honors Surya and takes its name from rice boiled with milk and jaggery.\n- **Cattle festival** (*Mattu Pongal*) — Cattle are bathed, decorated, garlanded and taken in processions.\n- **Bull-holding event** (*Jallikattu*) — A bull is released into a crowd and participants try to hold its hump.\n- **Tamil New Year** (*Puthandu*) — Puthandu marks the first day of the Tamil calendar in April.\n- **Festival of lights** (*Karthikai Deepam*) — This festival is observed on the full moon of the Kartika month in November or December.\n\n## Foodways\n\nRice is the staple, meals are often served on banana leaves, and hospitality is a major duty.\n\n- **Lentil and vegetable sauce** (*sambar*) — Sambar is commonly served with rice.\n- **Spiced broth** (*rasam*) — Rasam is commonly served with rice.\n- **Vegetable dish** (*poriyal*) — Poriyal is commonly served with rice.\n- **Steamed rice cake** (*idli*) — Idli is a popular Tamil dish.\n- **Rice pancake** (*dosa*) — Dosa is a popular Tamil dish.\n\n## Oral tradition & literature\n\nTamil literature is ancient and includes poetry, epics, ethics, religious hymns and storytelling.\n\n- **Grammar treatise** (*Tolkāppiyam*) — This is the oldest surviving book of Tamil literature and discusses Tamil grammar.\n- **Eight Anthologies** (*Ettuttokai*) — This is a collection of early Tamil literature.\n- **Ten Idylls** (*Pattuppattu*) — This is a collection of early Tamil poems.\n- **Ethics book** (*Tirukkural*) — This is a book on ethics by Thiruvalluvar.\n- **Epic of Kannagi** (*Cilappatikaram*) — This epic tells how Kannagi took revenge after her husband Kovalan was wrongly executed.\n\n## Language & religion\n\nTamil is an ancient Dravidian language, and Tamil religious life combines Hindu traditions with village worship and other faiths.\n\n- **Tamil language** (*Tamil*) — Tamil belongs to the southern Dravidian branch and is an official language in Tamil Nadu, Puducherry, Singapore and Sri Lanka.\n- **Tamil Brahmi script** (*Tamil Brahmi*) — This is one of the historical writing systems of Tamil.\n- **Rounded script** (*vaṭṭeḻuttu*) — This is one of the historical writing systems of Tamil.\n- **Village guardian deity** (*Ayyanar*) — Ayyanar is one of the named deities in Tamil religious life.\n- **Healing system** (*Siddha medicine*) — Siddha medicine is a traditional system associated with Tamils and Siddhars.\n\n## Glossary\n\n- *Tamilar* — Tamil people\n- *sari* — Draped garment\n- *pavaadai* — Long skirt\n- *dhavani* — Short sari\n- *veshti* — Men's wrapped cloth\n- *lungi* — Tubular wrapped garment\n- *garbhagriha* — Temple sanctum\n- *vimana* — Pyramidal temple tower\n- *mantapa* — Temple porch\n- *gopuram* — Ornate temple gate tower\n- *salangai* — Bell anklets\n- *veni* — Flower hair pattern\n- *Tamil Pannisai* — Ancient Tamil music system\n- *nadaswaram* — Temple reed instrument\n- *thavil* — Temple drum\n- *melam* — Percussion group\n- *Villu Paatu* — Bow-song storytelling\n- *Bharatanatyam* — Classical Tamil dance\n- *Koothu* — Street theatre\n- *Pongal* — Harvest festival and rice dish\n- *Jallikattu* — Bull-holding event\n- *Tolkāppiyam* — Tamil grammar book\n- *Tirukkural* — Book of ethics\n- *Siddha medicine* — Traditional Tamil healing system\n\n## Sources & further reading\n\n- [Tamils](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tamils)\n- [Tamil culture](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tamil_culture)\n- [Tamil art](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tamil_art)\n- [Tamil mythology](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tamil_mythology)\n- [Tamil language](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tamil_language)\n- [Tamil music](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tamil_music)\n- [Tamil cuisine](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tamil_cuisine)\n- The UNESCO Intangible Cultural Heritage source supplied for this profile lists no inscriptions for this country.\n- The museum catalogue records supplied for this profile do not provide collection URLs.\n",
